This narrowness is the point, not tidiness.  Every vendored file that SW-01 can
reach is Class C SOUP that has to be justified and anomaly-reviewed (NP-SW-CI-001
§9.4) — five of the seven headers here.  Register and intrinsic definitions are a
materially smaller review burden than the DSP/NN/RTOS/Driver bodies of logic, and
— see the anomaly review — it is also what makes the §7.1.2 finding as clean as it
is: essentially the whole published anomaly surface of CMSIS_5 lies in components
that are not here.  Adding the Cortex-M7 pair on 2026-09-01 did not widen that
burden, because neither file is on SW-01's include path; it did widen the set of
files a tag bump would have to be re-justified against, which is why the two
subsets are named separately above rather than merged into one list.

Standing obligation on the SW-02 subset (2026-09-01).  core_cm7.h and
cachel1_armv7.h carry NO §7.1.2 anomaly evaluation, because §9.3 obligation 5
attaches that to Class C SOUP and these two are compiled only into SW-02 (Class
B).  That is a claim about reachability, and it is checkable rather than asserted:
firmware/safety_mcu/ is a separate CMake project whose toolchain file and include
paths never mention MIMXRT1062, and core_cm0plus.h — the only CMSIS core header
SW-01 includes — does not include either file.  If a change ever makes either
file reachable from SW-01, obligation 5 applies to it and NP-SOUP-CMSIS-001 must
be revised before that change lands.  Moving the pinned tag re-triggers the
evaluation for the Class C subset regardless.
